description:
**Contract risk**: `Link` has two URI-providing fields:

    #[serde(skip_serializing_if = \"Option::is_none\")]
    pub href: Option<String>,

    #[serde(skip_serializing_if = \"Option::is_none\")]
    pub blob_id: Option<Id>,

RFC 8984 §1.4.11 says `href` is mandatory; the existing finding
JMAP-mno4.2 flags this as a spec deviation. The
draft-ietf-jmap-calendars §5.3 / §10.9.14 amendment makes
`blob_id` an acceptable substitute when stored as a JMAP blob.
The combined contract is therefore \"at least one of `href` and
`blob_id` MUST be present\".

The type system encodes neither side of this:

    let bad = Link {
        at_type: \"Link\".into(),
        href: None,
        blob_id: None,
        // ... all other Options None ...
        extra: serde_json::Map::new(),
    };
    // (this construction is only possible inside the crate due to
    // #[non_exhaustive], but the wire-deserialize path is open:)
    let bad2: Link = serde_json::from_value(json!({
        \"@type\": \"Link\"
    })).unwrap();
    // bad2.href.is_none() && bad2.blob_id.is_none()  — and the
    // type accepts it.

**What goes wrong for callers**:
- Every consumer that reads a `Link` must defensively write
  `link.href.as_ref().or(link.blob_id.as_ref().map(...))` or
  equivalent. The compiler offers no help; missing the check
  produces a value-less Link silently.
- Code that builds a `Link` to send out can ship a no-source Link
  by mistake. Server-side validation might catch it, or might not.
- This is the canonical case of a defensive-code-forcing API:
  100% of correct consumers will write the same is-it-set check
  forever.

**Fix shape** (breaking change):
Option A: sum type for the source field:

    #[non_exhaustive]
    pub enum LinkSource {
        Href(String),
        BlobId(Id),
        // future-extensible
    }

    pub struct Link {
        pub source: LinkSource,
        // ... rest of fields ...
    }

Wire-format mapping done via a custom serde flatten: serialize
`Href(s)` as `{\"href\": s}`, `BlobId(id)` as `{\"blobId\": id}`.
This encodes the invariant and lets callers `match link.source`.

Option B: keep the current shape but ship a helper:

    impl Link {
        pub fn uri(&self) -> Option<LinkSource<'_>> { ... }
    }

…where `LinkSource<'_>` is a borrowed view. Non-breaking; partially
addresses the defensive-code burden but does not prevent
construction of source-less Links.

Option C: leave as-is, document the contract in the doc-comment
\"a well-formed Link MUST have exactly one of `href` and `blob_id`;
this type permits the absent case for round-trip preservation.\"
Cheapest; still leaves the burden on consumers.

This bead is filed at P1 because it is the strongest defensive-code-
forcing instance in this crate's API and will be repeated across
every consumer.
